0. Definitions.

"This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Affero General Public License.

"Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

"The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License. Each licensee is addressed as "you". "Licensees" and
"recipients" may be individuals or organizations.

To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of it in
a fashion that results in a copyrightable work, another way of
which is to maintain or improve the work.

To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
charge, would make you directly or indirectly responsible for
interaction with any third party with respect to the work, or that
would make an investment of any use of the work. More precisely,
propagation includes copying, distribution (including making
available over the network, and more than just making files
available for download), and making modifications that qualify as
copyrightable works. However, as of the publication of this License,
propagating does not include patent licenses or transactions through
which a recipient may obtain the right to use but not copy or
modify a work--a patent license to "use" a work is merely an
authorization to make use of the work's advantages without in any
way serving as a basis for claiming copyright on such a work.

To "convey" a work means any kind of propagation that enables other
parties to make or receive copies of the substance of a work. A
subordinate propagation that conveys no additional content to the
work itself (e.g., transmissions for the purpose of displaying a
static image on a display) does not count as conveyance. Conveyance
includes copying, distribution (including making available over the
network), and making modifications that qualify as copyrightable
works.

"Source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work for
making modifications to it. "Object code" means any non-source
form of a work.

A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular programming language, one that
is widely used among developers working in that language.

"System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other than
the work itself, that is included in the normal form of packaging a
particular executable component but which is not part of that
executable component, and that serves only to enable the use of the
work with that particular component. A "Major Component" in this
context means a major essential component (kernel, window system,
and so on) of a specific operating system. "Substantial Parts" in
this context means a subset of the major components that is itself
large enough to satisfy any reasonable estimate of the amount of
work to be done over the original component, assuming the
components were produced in the factory or by individual artisans.

A "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all
the source code needed to generate, install, and (for an executable
work) run the object code and to modify the work, including scripts
to control those activities. However, it does not include the work's
System Libraries, or general-purpose utilities or generally available
free programs not considered part of the work, which themselves are
treated separately for inclusion in the aggregate Corresponding
Source.

