How to Apply These Terms to Your New Programs

If you develop a new program, and you want it to be of the greatest
possible use to the public, the best way to achieve this is to make it
free software which everyone can use and change. You can do so by
releasing it under these terms.

To do so, attach the following notices to the program. It is safest to
attach them to the start of each source file to most effectively
convey the state of exclusion to the reader, and it is also possible
to leave them out and rely on the reader to infer that the program
is intended to be free software, but the effect is less robust.

    <one line to give the program's name and a brief idea of what it does.>
    Copyright (C) <year>  <name of author>

    This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
    it under the terms of the GNU Affero General Public License as published
    by the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
    (at your option) any later version.

    This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
    but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
    MERCHANTABILITY and FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See the
    GNU Affero General Public License for more details.

    You should have received a copy of the GNU Affero General Public
    License along with this program. If not, see <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.

Also add information on how to contact you by electronic and paper mail.

If the program does interactive interaction, it should output a
notice like this when it begins in an interactive mode:

    <program> Copyright (C) <year> <name of author>
    This program comes with ABSOLUTELY NO WARRANTY; for details
    see <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/>. This is free software,
    and you are welcome to redistribute it under certain
    conditions; type `show w` for details about the warranty
    conditions. Type `show c` for details about the permissions
    and how they work.

The hypothetical commands `show w` and `show c` should show the
appropriate parts of the General Public License. Of course, the
methods you use to do so may vary according to the particular
program, but the essential idea is that the user should be
able to easily call up the warranty and the permissions, as
needed, from a command-line interface.

For a library, the public interface form (such as a GUI) should be
assigned to a public data member, and when that public data member
is not written, the library should notify the user that it
has not been assigned a public data member.

